WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.928
Here's a web request handler, written in Python.

00:00:03.978 --> 00:00:11.249
It needs a user and that user's orders, so it asks two services at the same time, with asyncio.gather.

00:00:11.499 --> 00:00:13.619
The user service takes one second.

00:00:13.669 --> 00:00:16.786
The orders service fails after a tenth of a second.

00:00:17.036 --> 00:00:20.519
So the handler returns an error at a tenth of a second.

00:00:20.569 --> 00:00:22.502
So far, that's what you'd want.

00:00:22.752 --> 00:00:25.608
But the request to the user service is still running.

00:00:25.658 --> 00:00:31.067
It finishes a full second after the request began, long after anyone was waiting for it.

00:00:32.317 --> 00:00:35.038
And if it had failed, its error would go nowhere.

00:00:35.088 --> 00:00:37.696
Nothing raises it, and nothing logs it.

00:00:38.746 --> 00:00:41.135
Now serve a thousand requests at once.

00:00:41.185 --> 00:00:46.569
All thousand handlers return almost at once, and a thousand tasks are still running.

00:00:47.619 --> 00:00:49.073
The function returned.

00:00:49.123 --> 00:00:50.851
Why is its work still running?

00:00:50.901 --> 00:00:53.168
And what would make "returned" mean "done"?

00:00:56.918 --> 00:01:02.047
Start with plain code: fetch the user, then fetch the orders, one after the other.

00:01:02.297 --> 00:01:04.985
Each call has one way in and one way out.

00:01:05.035 --> 00:01:07.350
When fetch_user returns, it's finished.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:10.152
If it fails, the error comes back to the caller.

00:01:10.202 --> 00:01:18.955
And if the caller gives up, say the client hangs up, the server cancels it: it asks the code to stop, and the call it's waiting on stops too.

00:01:19.205 --> 00:01:22.880
So when the handler returns, everything it started is done.

00:01:22.930 --> 00:01:24.953
You can treat it as a black box.

00:01:25.203 --> 00:01:26.248
But it's slow.

00:01:26.298 --> 00:01:30.980
The calls wait for each other, so the error arrives after one point one seconds.

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:34.822
That's why we start the two requests at the same time.

00:01:35.772 --> 00:01:38.814
Starting a task is different from calling a function.

00:01:38.864 --> 00:01:42.704
The start returns at once, and the task runs on by itself.

00:01:42.754 --> 00:01:47.151
Control splits into two paths, and nothing makes the second one come back.

00:01:48.201 --> 00:01:52.078
There are two common ways to wait for tasks, and both leave gaps.

00:01:52.328 --> 00:01:53.794
The first is gather.

00:01:53.844 --> 00:01:56.318
You'd expect it to behave like those calls.

00:01:56.368 --> 00:02:00.345
It waits for both results, and when both requests succeed, it does.

00:02:00.595 --> 00:02:07.294
But when one fails, gather by default passes that error on immediately, and doesn't cancel the other task.

00:02:07.344 --> 00:02:09.652
Python's own documentation says so.

00:02:11.102 --> 00:02:15.558
That's the request left running in the opening. gather waited for results.

00:02:15.608 --> 00:02:17.278
It never owned the tasks.

00:02:17.528 --> 00:02:23.478
The second way is to start both requests, then await them one at a time, the user request first.

00:02:23.528 --> 00:02:30.073
If the client gives up after half a second, the user request is cancelled, because it's the one being awaited.

00:02:30.123 --> 00:02:33.221
The orders request isn't, and runs on to the end.

00:02:34.471 --> 00:02:36.753
So a real fix has to do three things.

00:02:36.803 --> 00:02:38.765
Wait for every task it starts.

00:02:38.815 --> 00:02:42.114
When one fails, cancel the rest and pass the error on.

00:02:42.164 --> 00:02:44.595
And when the caller gives up, cancel them all.

00:02:46.345 --> 00:02:48.874
This problem is older than concurrency.

00:02:48.924 --> 00:02:53.587
In 1968, Edsger Dijkstra argued against the go to statement.

00:02:53.637 --> 00:02:59.357
With jumps that can lead anywhere, it becomes hard to follow what a program is doing from its text.

00:02:59.607 --> 00:03:08.405
The fix was structured programming: blocks, conditionals, loops and function calls, where control goes in at the top and comes out at the bottom.

00:03:08.655 --> 00:03:15.645
Fifty years later, in 2018, Nathaniel Smith pointed out that starting a task is the same kind of jump.

00:03:15.695 --> 00:03:22.472
Control goes in, and part of it never has to come out, so you can no longer treat a function as a black box.

00:03:22.522 --> 00:03:28.162
His essay was called "Notes on structured concurrency, or: Go statement considered harmful".

00:03:29.412 --> 00:03:31.769
The fix is the same too: a block.

00:03:31.819 --> 00:03:34.204
Tasks started inside it can't outlive it.

00:03:34.254 --> 00:03:37.819
The block doesn't end until every task in it has finished.

00:03:38.869 --> 00:03:44.895
That rule is called structured concurrency, a name Martin Sústrik had given it two years earlier.

00:03:44.945 --> 00:03:55.069
Smith built it into his own library, Trio, and Python's asyncio later added the same kind of block, the task group, in version three point eleven.

00:03:57.019 --> 00:04:01.401
Here's the handler again, with both requests started inside a task group.

00:04:01.651 --> 00:04:04.450
The orders service fails at a tenth of a second.

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:10.777
At once, the task group cancels the user request, which asks it to stop, and waits until it has.

00:04:11.627 --> 00:04:13.969
Then it raises the error in the handler.

00:04:14.019 --> 00:04:20.151
It arrives inside an exception group, a container for errors, since more than one task could fail.

00:04:20.201 --> 00:04:22.861
So a plain except ConnectionError won't match it.

00:04:22.911 --> 00:04:26.865
The handler catches it with except star, which looks inside the group.

00:04:27.915 --> 00:04:32.772
At a tenth of a second the handler returns, and nothing it started is still running.

00:04:32.822 --> 00:04:36.183
So nothing is left to fail later, where no one would hear it.

00:04:36.433 --> 00:04:40.930
A thousand requests: all the handlers return, and no tasks are left.

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:49.274
And if the client gives up after half a second, cancelling the handler cancels both requests inside it, not just the one being awaited.

00:04:50.524 --> 00:04:52.895
So a task group gives three guarantees.

00:04:52.945 --> 00:04:55.465
The block waits for every task started in it.

00:04:55.515 --> 00:04:59.666
When one fails, the others are cancelled and the error reaches the caller.

00:04:59.716 --> 00:05:02.300
And cancelling the caller cancels its tasks.

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:06.736
A task group can only ask a task to stop.

00:05:06.786 --> 00:05:09.495
The request arrives at the task's next await.

00:05:09.545 --> 00:05:12.479
It arrives as an exception, called CancelledError.

00:05:12.529 --> 00:05:17.891
A task that never awaits, or that catches that exception and carries on, keeps running.

00:05:18.141 --> 00:05:25.946
Here's a retry loop with a bare except: an except with no exception type, so it catches everything, cancellation included.

00:05:25.996 --> 00:05:31.553
When the orders service fails, the user request catches its cancellation and tries again.

00:05:31.803 --> 00:05:33.743
The task group has to wait for it.

00:05:33.793 --> 00:05:38.009
The handler returns after one point one seconds, instead of a tenth.

00:05:39.259 --> 00:05:43.219
Before, the stubborn work ran on after the handler returned.

00:05:43.269 --> 00:05:47.003
Now it can't escape the block, so the handler waits for it instead.

00:05:47.253 --> 00:05:52.616
And the rule covers only tasks started through the task group, with its own create_task.

00:05:52.666 --> 00:05:57.579
A task started with plain asyncio.create_task is on its own again.

00:05:57.829 --> 00:06:00.546
And it's about lifetimes, not shared data.

00:06:00.596 --> 00:06:04.861
Two tasks in the same group can still race on a variable, or deadlock.

00:06:06.411 --> 00:06:08.422
But this isn't a quirk of Python.

00:06:08.472 --> 00:06:11.899
Here's the same handler in Go, with plain goroutines.

00:06:11.949 --> 00:06:21.823
After a thousand requests, a thousand goroutines are left, and they never finish: each is waiting to hand its result to a handler that has already returned.

00:06:22.873 --> 00:06:25.103
Go has no task group in the language.

00:06:25.153 --> 00:06:32.483
A common convention is an error group, from Go's extended libraries: the closest thing Go has to a task group.

00:06:32.733 --> 00:06:39.955
An error group works with a context: Go's standard way of passing cancellation and deadlines to goroutines.

00:06:40.205 --> 00:06:42.301
With an error group, none are left.

00:06:42.951 --> 00:06:48.729
The first error cancels the context, and here each goroutine checks the context and returns.

00:06:48.779 --> 00:06:51.903
Like a task group, an error group can only ask.

00:06:52.153 --> 00:06:57.473
Swift and Java have the same kind of block built in, Java's still as a preview feature.

00:06:57.523 --> 00:07:00.664
Kotlin has it in its official coroutines library.

00:07:02.214 --> 00:07:05.616
So why was the handler's work still running after it returned?

00:07:05.666 --> 00:07:14.993
Because starting a task split control into two paths, and nothing made the second one come back. gather waited for the results, but it didn't own the tasks.

00:07:15.243 --> 00:07:21.527
Structured concurrency gives every task an owner: a block that can't end until its tasks have.

00:07:21.577 --> 00:07:26.264
In a task group, when one fails, the rest are cancelled and the error comes back.

00:07:26.314 --> 00:07:28.943
When the caller is cancelled, so are its tasks.

00:07:29.193 --> 00:07:34.589
The request fails at a tenth of a second, and at a tenth of a second nothing is left running.

00:07:34.839 --> 00:07:38.070
Give every task an owner, and "returned" means "done".
